  "body": "Draft for the maintainer's review (2026-11-10: \"we need a way of 'weighting' an issue ... launched with the matching agent - this to manage token spend on different levels of complexity of tickets\").\n\n## What changes\n- **AGENTS.md, Issue Law: new \"Every issue is weighted by effort\".**\n  - **Labels:** one `effort:low|medium|high|max` label per issue, beside `P*`, rating the *work*, not a model. A table gives the examples.\n  - **When it's set:** at filing. A human-filed issue gets one at its first triage.\n  - **Safety floor:** anything that can change posted G-code, a collision or gouge check, stock simulation or geometry is at least `effort:high`, and so is every P0.\n  - **Who works it:** `P*` still decides *what* is worked next, and `effort:*` decides *who* works it. The main session dispatches the issue to `.claude/agents/issue-worker-<effort>.md` and keeps the review and merge decisions.\n  - **Review tier:** reviews run at least on the medium tier, and on high for anything under the safety floor.\n  - **Escalation:** a worker that fails its quick gate twice, or finds the work bigger than its label, stops. It comments what it learned, raises the label one step and releases the claim. One escalation per run; only the maintainer lowers a label.\n  - **The other label lists:** `needs-research` sub-issues, follow-up issues and agent-filed issues now include `effort:*`.\n- **`.claude/agents/issue-worker-{low,medium,high,max}.md`:** four thin agents. Their only difference is `model:` (haiku / sonnet / opus / fable) and which labels they accept. The model choice lives only here, so a fork on other models edits four lines. A 404 on a model alias means running without `model:` and saying so in the claim comment.\n- **`.claude/skills/work-issue/SKILL.md`:** the shared worker procedure.\n  - **Pre-checks:** `P?`, needs-research, blocked, a live claim, or an effort above the worker's tier mean it stops.\n  - **Steps:** claim, then a worktree from the latest main, then the fix with a pinning test, then the quick gate.\n  - **Escalation:** the rule above.\n  - **Finish:** a draft PR with an `Effort:` line, the issue moved to `in-review`, and the worktree removed. It never merges, never marks the PR ready and never reviews its own work.\n- **`.claude/skills/review-prs/SKILL.md` step 2c:** the cost comment adds `Effort: <label>` and any escalation, the data for recalibrating the table.\n- **`.claude/PROCESS_VERSION`:** 5 → 6.\n\n## Not in this PR (after merge)\n- **Labels:** create `effort:low`, `effort:medium`, `effort:high` and `effort:max`.\n- **Tagging pass:** one pass over the open issues proposes and applies an effort label to each from the table, as a separate step the maintainer can correct.\n- **Announcement:** a `PROCESS_VERSION 6` note on #681.\n- **gh-issues skill:** the local skill (outside the repo) gets the same filing rule.\n\n## Open questions for review\n- **Tier mapping:** low=Haiku is the lowest bar. On this codebase I expect it to fit only truly mechanical issues. Keep it, or start `low` on Sonnet?\n- **Safety floor:** high is the proposed level. Should it be `max` for collision and gouge laws?\n- **Escalation trigger:** \"two failed quick gates\" is a guess. The cost data will show whether it's right.\n\nChangelog: none\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
